<p>Most aftercare sheets read as though every patient goes home in a car, works at a desk, and lives somewhere the weather is a background detail. Here it is not. A scooter and a mandatory helmet, a May afternoon at 44&deg;C, a service road being dug up outside your sector and eight weeks of monsoon humidity all press on the part of you that is healing. None of it changes the surgery. It changes how hard the first month is, and it is one of the few things still in your hands, because you also choose the date.</p>

<h2 id="helmets-and-the-two-wheeler-commute">Helmets and a commute you cannot legally skip</h2>

<p>For the first days the grafts sit in shallow incisions, held largely by clotting and swelling, and they become steadily more secure over the following week or two. That is why nearly every clinic restricts pressure, rubbing and friction across that window. The <a href="/hair-transplant-aftercare">standard aftercare instructions given to patients</a> set out the principle; a helmet is where it meets a Gurgaon commute.</p>

<p>A full-face helmet is the worst case. Pulling it on drags the padded rim across the frontal recipient area, exactly where the newest grafts are, and the rear rim then sits on the donor zone, which after <a href="/fue-hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">FUE extraction across the back and sides</a> is a field of small healing punctures. Trapped heat and sweat under the lining complete the picture.</p>

<p>Riding without one is not a workaround: helmets are legally required in Haryana, and a head injury on a healing scalp is far worse than a delayed graft. If your only transport is a two-wheeler, plan not to use it for roughly the first two weeks, and have that timeline confirmed for your own case.</p>

<h3>If you genuinely cannot avoid riding</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Take your leave in the first fortnight rather than saving it. This is the stretch where being absent protects something.</li>
  <li>Arrange cabs or a carpool for the days you must travel, and budget for that before surgery.</li>
  <li>Once cleared, return on an open-face helmet a size larger, lowered vertically onto the head rather than dragged forward.</li>
  <li>Ride when you will sweat least, and take the helmet off on arrival.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="heat-sweat-and-direct-sun">Heat, sweat and sun on a healing scalp</h2>

<p>From late April to the end of June, an outdoor errand here means a soaked scalp. Sweat itself does not lift grafts, but it softens crusts early, makes the area itch, and creates a strong urge to wipe or scratch, which does move things. Salt on raw skin also stings enough that people start improvising remedies, which is how avoidable problems begin.</p>

<p>Sun is the second issue. The skin over the recipient area is new, thin and unusually sun-sensitive for several weeks, and a burn there is painful and slow to settle. Until the clinic clears a covering, the answer is shade and timing: outdoor tasks early or after sunset, and air conditioning through the afternoon.</p>

<p>Some swelling of the forehead and around the eyes in the first days is common, settles on its own, and feels worse in heat. Telling it apart from something needing attention is covered in the guide to <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-side-effects-and-risks">expected side effects and the uncommon complications</a>.</p>

<h2 id="construction-dust-and-ncr-air">Construction dust and the NCR particulate load</h2>

<p>A fresh recipient area is, in practical terms, several hundred very small open wounds. Dust is an irritant on it and a contamination risk in it. Gurgaon supplies dust generously: construction across the newer sectors, unpaved edges beside main roads, pre-monsoon storms off the Aravallis, and a winter inversion that holds particulate matter at head height for weeks.</p>

<p>Shielding sensibly means avoiding the exposure rather than wrapping the head. A cloth tied over grafts traps heat, presses on the follicles and is usually dirtier than the air it filters. The better measures are ordinary: car windows closed with the air recirculating, distance from cutting and grinding work, no standing on the balcony facing the site next door, and dust that does settle left for the scheduled wash rather than rubbed off. If your work is genuinely dusty, that is a reason to book leave, not to buy a cover. Air quality is blamed for a great deal here, and <a href="/blog/hard-water-pollution-and-hair-fall-in-gurgaon">what hard water and pollution actually do to hair</a> is narrower than the salon version.</p>

<h2 id="monsoon-humidity-and-folliculitis">Monsoon humidity, occlusion and folliculitis</h2>

<p>July to September is the awkward season. Sweat does not evaporate, anything on your head stays damp, and a warm, occluded scalp is hospitable to the bacteria already living on it. Folliculitis, meaning small tender pustules around emerging hairs, is a recognised occurrence in the weeks after a transplant, and humidity plus a helmet plus a cap makes it likelier. It is usually minor and treatable, but it needs to be seen rather than squeezed, because picking at a pustule risks the graft inside it. Spreading redness, increasing pain, warmth or discharge is a same-day call to the clinic.</p>

<p>Monsoon also overlaps the normal shedding phase, so patients in a wet August often decide the surgery has failed at the moment nothing is wrong. <a href="/blog/shedding-and-shock-loss-after-hair-transplant">Why transplanted hairs fall out around week three</a> is worth reading first, alongside <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-growth-timeline-month-by-month">what each month of regrowth looks like</a>. A scalp under physical stress can also shed diffusely for reasons unrelated to the grafts, a pattern described in DermNet's account of <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/telogen-effluvium" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">telogen effluvium</a>.</p>

<h2 id="gym-cricket-and-swimming">Gym, cricket and swimming: a staged return</h2>

<p>Patients want a single date for the diary, and activity does not work that way. Sweat, straining, direct impact and immersion are four separate problems with four separate timelines.</p>

<table class="data">
  <caption>Typical staging, subject to your surgeon's review</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr><th scope="col">Activity</th><th scope="col">Usually earliest</th><th scope="col">The reason</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Gentle walking</td><td>Within days</td><td>Little sweat, no straining</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Light indoor cardio</td><td>Around two weeks</td><td>Sweat over a settling area</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weights, heavy lifting</td><td>Three to four weeks</td><td>Straining can worsen swelling</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cricket, football, outdoor running</td><td>Around a month</td><td>Sun, dust, a stray impact</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Chlorinated pool</td><td>Four to six weeks</td><td>Irritation of remodelling skin</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Contact sport, or sport in a helmet</td><td>Later, and reviewed</td><td>Trauma and pressure together</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Those are bands, not promises, and an examination can move any of them either way. A cricket helmet carries the motorcycle helmet problem with impact added, so anyone whose sport involves headgear should raise it at the consultation, not at the first follow-up.</p>

<h2 id="going-back-to-work">Going back to work: desk roles against field roles</h2>

<p>How soon you return depends less on healing than on who has to look at you. Redness lasts a couple of weeks and fades gradually, crusting is visible for the first ten days or so, and the donor area is trimmed short. None of that is dangerous, and all of it is noticeable at conversational distance.</p>

<p>At a desk, with your own diary, many patients are functional within a week and accept some pinkness. Client-facing, presenting or on camera, two to three weeks is more comfortable. On a site or in the field in summer, with hard hats, heat, dust and long hours away from anywhere clean, three to four weeks or a reassignment agreed in advance is the usual answer. Nobody can promise you will look unremarkable by a named date; a clinic can only describe the ordinary range. The same impatience distorts how people judge results later, which is why <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">reading a before-and-after photograph properly</a> matters.</p>

