<h2 id="choosing-a-month-to-book">Choosing a month, and the months that make it harder</h2>

<p>If your dates are flexible, the weather is worth planning around. None of this is a clinical contraindication, and transplants are performed here all year, indoors, in a controlled room. It is only about how hard the first four weeks are to live through.</p>

<h3>The easier windows</h3>

<p>Late September to early December, and again from late January into March, are the kindest stretches. You sweat less, you can be outdoors without planning around shade, and crusts are not softening every afternoon. Early winter carries one caveat: NCR air quality is typically at its worst from late October into December, which matters for dust avoidance rather than for the grafts.</p>

<h3>The harder windows</h3>

<p>May and June are hardest, purely for sweat and sun, and they are when a two-wheeler commute becomes genuinely awkward. July to mid-September brings the humidity problem above. If those are the only months open to you, recovery is still manageable. It asks more of your discipline, and it makes taking the full leave period more important rather than less.</p>

<h2 id="planning-around-fixed-dates">Planning around weddings, Holi and travel</h2>

<p>The commonest scheduling error is booking surgery too close to an event. Your hair looks worse at one to three months than it did beforehand, because the transplanted hairs shed before they regrow, and a result is honestly judged at around ten to twelve months. If a wedding or a photographed occasion matters, count backwards a year, not a season.</p>

<p>Holi deserves a mention. Colours, water balloons and vigorous washing are all things you do not want near a recipient area, and the scrubbing needed to remove the colour is worse than the colour. If it falls inside the first six weeks, sit it out or move the surgery. For travel, the constraint is usually the follow-up rather than the flight: you have washes to learn, a review to attend and a clinic you may want to reach quickly. Dusty destinations, hotel pools and beach sun are easier a month or two later.</p>

<p>Then there is the lead time before surgery, which people underestimate. A consultation, blood work and often a period of medical treatment to stabilise ongoing loss come first, and for some people the answer at that stage is not surgery at all, as set out in <a href="/blog/who-should-not-have-a-hair-transplant">the cases where a transplant is the wrong answer</a>. Then a date, then the recovery window: think in months. That stage is also where the practical questions belong, whether that is <a href="/hair-transplant-cost-in-gurgaon">what a hair transplant costs in Gurgaon</a>, the available <a href="/cost-and-emi-options">payment and EMI arrangements</a>, or the technique, since <a href="/dhi-hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">placement with an implanter pen</a> changes nothing about heat or helmets.</p>

<p>Bring your calendar to the consultation along with your questions. The <a href="/blog/questions-to-ask-hair-transplant-consultation">questions worth asking before you commit</a> cover the clinical side; the date is the part only you can supply. You can read <a href="/dr-nyra">Dr. Nyra's qualifications and registration</a> beforehand, and the overview of <a href="/hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">hair transplant surgery at the Sector 39 clinic</a> sets out the day itself.</p>

